//? Title: Aho-Corasick: Matching a Whole Dictionary in One Pass
//? Subtitle: Trie automata, failure links and a linear-time proof, applied to the Titanic passenger list with datamunge's algorithms::AhoCorasick
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Searching a text of length \(n\) for each of \(k\) patterns separately costs
\(O(k\,n)\). Aho and Corasick compile the whole dictionary into one
automaton: a trie of the patterns, with failure links that generalise the
Knuth-Morris-Pratt prefix function, and dictionary links. Reading the text
once then finds every occurrence. We prove the failure-link recursion
correct and show that a scan follows at most \(n\) failure links, so it
costs \(O(n+z)\) for \(z\) matches, whatever \(k\) is. The textbook
dictionary \{he, she, his, hers\} is traced state by state. On the 891
names of `dm::datasets::titanic()`, a dictionary of 17 honorifics extracts
every passenger's title in a single pass. The titles separate survival
rates from 16% (Mr) to 70% and more (Miss, Mrs). A dictionary of up to 660
surnames keeps the automaton under 0.7 transitions per character, against a
proven bound of 2. Its search time at most triples while pattern-by-pattern
KMP's rises about 500-fold.
)

\toc

> The problem and the naive cost

Given a text \(T\) of length \(n\) and patterns \(P_0,\dots,P_{k-1}\) of
total length \(m\), report every pair \((i,j)\) such that \(P_j\) occurs in
\(T\) starting at \(i\). This includes overlapping occurrences and patterns
that are suffixes of other patterns. Running KMP once per pattern costs
\(O(n+|P_j|)\) each, \(O(kn+m)\) in total: the text is read \(k\) times.
Aho-Corasick reads it once.

> The automaton

**States** are the nodes of the trie of the patterns: one state per
distinct prefix \(w\) of some pattern, with the root as the empty prefix.
The goto edge \(w\xrightarrow{c}wc\) exists when \(wc\) is also a prefix.
So there are at most \(1+m\) states.

**Failure link.** For a state \(w\ne\varepsilon\), \(f(w)\) is the longest
*proper* suffix of \(w\) that is also a state.

**Dictionary link.** \(d(w)\) is the first state on the chain
\(f(w), f(f(w)),\dots\) at which some pattern ends.

The scan keeps the invariant that, after reading \(T[0..i]\), the current
state is the **longest suffix of \(T[0..i]\) that is a state**. Every
pattern ending at \(i\) is a suffix of the current state, and so lies on
its failure chain. The dictionary links visit exactly those states, at
\(O(1)\) per pattern reported.

>> Computing the failure links

**Lemma.** Let \(u\xrightarrow{c}v\) be a trie edge, so \(v=uc\). Let \(g\)
be the deepest state on the chain \(f(u), f^2(u),\dots,\varepsilon\) that
has a \(c\)-edge. Then \(f(v)=gc\), or \(\varepsilon\) if no state on the
chain has a \(c\)-edge.

*Proof.* Any proper suffix of \(v=uc\) that is a state, other than
\(\varepsilon\), has the form \(sc\), where \(s\) is a proper suffix of
\(u\). Because states are closed under taking prefixes, \(s\) is itself a
state. The proper suffixes of \(u\) that are states are exactly the states
on the failure chain of \(u\), by induction on the definition of \(f\). The
longest valid \(sc\) therefore uses the deepest \(s\) on that chain with a
\(c\)-edge. \(\square\)

The lemma refers only to shallower states, so the links can be filled in
breadth-first order. Similarly, \(d(v)=f(v)\) if a pattern ends at
\(f(v)\), and \(d(v)=d(f(v))\) otherwise.

The same argument shows that the scan keeps its invariant. Reading \(c\)
from state \(w\), the search walks the failure chain of \(w\) to the
deepest state with a \(c\)-edge and takes it.

>> The scan is linear

Take the depth of the current state, \(|w|\), as a potential. A goto step
raises it by exactly 1. A failure step lowers it by at least 1, because
\(f(w)\) is a proper suffix. The potential starts at 0 and never goes
negative, so

$$
\#\text{failure steps}\;\le\;\#\text{goto steps}\;\le\;n ,
$$
\alttext(The number of failure steps is at most the number of goto steps, which is at most n.)

The scan therefore does at most \(2n\) transitions, plus one dictionary
link per match: \(O(n+z)\) time, with no dependence on \(k\). The same
potential argument, applied to each pattern as it is inserted, bounds the
breadth-first construction by \(O(m)\). Note that \(z\) can be large:
\(\{a, aa, \dots, a^k\}\) in \(a^n\) has \(z\approx kn\) matches, and any
algorithm must spend that time just reporting them.

`algorithms::AhoCorasick` builds the automaton once. Its `search`,
`count`, `longest_at` and `scan_stats` methods reuse it for any number of
texts, and `automaton()` returns it as a table.

> Worked example: \{he, she, his, hers\}

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  const dm::algorithms::AhoCorasick ac({"he", "she", "his", "hers"});
  std::print("{} states.\n\n", ac.state_count());
  std::print("{}\n", ac.automaton() | dm::to_md(0));

  std::print("Scan of \"ushers\":\n\n| match | pattern | start |\n| ---: | :--- | ---: |\n");
  int k = 0;
  for (const auto& m : ac.search("ushers"))
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n", ++k, ac.patterns()[m.pattern_index], m.position);
  const auto stats = ac.scan_stats("ushers");
  std::print("\n{} characters, {} goto steps, {} failure steps, {} matches\n", stats.characters, stats.goto_steps,
             stats.failure_steps, stats.matches);
}
```
