| 1 | she | 1 |
| 2 | he | 2 |
| 3 | hers | 2 |

6 characters, 5 goto steps, 1 failure steps, 3 matches
// result_end
\caption(The automaton for \{he, she, his, hers\} in breadth-first order, and its scan of "ushers")

The table shows the one interesting failure link, `she` → `he`. Because
`he` is a pattern, it is also `she`'s dictionary link. The scan of
`u s h e r s` runs as follows:

1. `u` has no edge from the root, so the scan stays at the root.
2. `s`, `h`, `e` reach state `she`. That state reports `she`, and its
   dictionary link reports `he`: two matches end at index 3.
3. `r` has no edge from `she`, so the scan follows one failure link to `he`
   and then takes the edge to `her`.
4. `s` reaches `hers`, the third match.

That is one failure step and five goto steps, within the bound.

> Application: titles on the Titanic passenger list

Each name in `dm::datasets::titanic()` reads "Surname, Title. Given names",
as in "Braund, Mr. Owen Harris". A dictionary of the 17 honorifics in the
list, each written as ", Title.", finds every title in one pass over all
891 names. The comma and the full stop stop "Mr" matching inside "Mrs".
Married women's names often contain a second, maiden-name title in
brackets, so each passenger takes the *first* match, the one at the
smallest position.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/aho_corasick_titles.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::vector<std::string> titles{"Mr", "Mrs", "Miss", "Master", "Dr", "Rev", "Col", "Major", "Mlle",
                                        "Mme", "Ms", "Capt", "Don", "Lady", "Sir", "Jonkheer", "the Countess"};
  std::vector<std::string> dictionary;
  for (const auto& t : titles) dictionary.push_back(", " + t + ".");
  const dm::algorithms::AhoCorasick ac(dictionary);

  auto titanic = dm::datasets::titanic();
  std::vector<std::string> title;
  for (std::size_t r = 0; r < titanic.nrows(); ++r) {
    const auto matches = ac.search(titanic.string_at("Name", r));
    const auto first = std::ranges::min_element(matches, {}, &dm::algorithms::AhoCorasickMatch::position);
    title.push_back(first == matches.end() ? "none" : titles[first->pattern_index]);
  }
  titanic.add_column("Title", title);

  const auto by_title = titanic | dm::group_by("Title") |
                        dm::summarise(dm::agg::n("passengers"), dm::agg::mean("Survived", "survival"),
                                      dm::agg::mean("Age", "mean_age")) |
                        dm::arrange(dm::desc("passengers"));
  std::print("{}\n", by_title | dm::to_md(2));

  // Plot the five titles with at least 7 passengers.
  std::vector<double> rate;
  std::vector<std::string> names;
  for (std::size_t r = 0; r < by_title.nrows(); ++r)
    if (by_title.double_at("passengers", r) >= 7) {
      names.push_back(by_title.string_at("Title", r) + " (" +
                      std::to_string(static_cast<int>(by_title.double_at("passengers", r))) + ")");
      rate.push_back(by_title.double_at("survival", r));
    }
  dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(rate, names, "survival rate", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235})
      .y_limits(0, 1)
      .title("Titanic survival by title (passengers in brackets)")
      .x_label("title")
      .y_label("proportion survived")
      .size(860, 480)
      .save_svg("build/aho_corasick_titles.svg");
}
```
