Text: 24917 bytes; 667 distinct surnames

| k | pattern bytes m | states | matches z | transitions / char | failure / char | AC ms | KMP ms |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 6 | 7 | 2 | 0.013 | 0.006 | 0.05 | 0.01 |
| 2 | 13 | 14 | 3 | 0.027 | 0.013 | 0.06 | 0.03 |
| 5 | 35 | 36 | 8 | 0.078 | 0.035 | 0.10 | 0.08 |
| 10 | 68 | 68 | 24 | 0.217 | 0.096 | 0.12 | 0.17 |
| 20 | 153 | 145 | 54 | 0.300 | 0.115 | 0.18 | 0.33 |
| 50 | 364 | 328 | 104 | 0.387 | 0.143 | 0.19 | 0.80 |
| 100 | 705 | 589 | 195 | 0.441 | 0.145 | 0.19 | 1.60 |
| 200 | 1365 | 1042 | 408 | 0.510 | 0.150 | 0.23 | 3.20 |
| 400 | 2762 | 1962 | 737 | 0.626 | 0.163 | 0.27 | 6.41 |
| 660 | 4580 | 3094 | 1118 | 0.693 | 0.160 | 0.29 | 10.59 |
\image(figures/aho_corasick_scaling.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Searching the names stream for the first \(k\) surnames. The automaton grows with the dictionary, but its work per character stays well below the proven bound of 2. KMP's time grows in proportion to \(k\). Both methods find the same matches at every \(k\))
\alttext(Two panels. Left: log time against log k. The red KMP line rises with slope about 1, from 0.02 to about 11 milliseconds; the blue Aho-Corasick line stays between about 0.1 and 0.3 milliseconds and crosses the KMP line near k equals 10. Right: transitions per character climb from 0.01 to 0.69, far under a grey line at 2.)

Both methods report the same matches at every \(k\).

- **KMP grows with \(k\).** Its time rises about 500-fold, from 0.02 to about 11 ms, as
  the dictionary grows 660-fold. That is the \(O(kn)\) cost, with slope 1
  on the log-log plot.
- **Aho-Corasick barely grows.** Its time stays between about 0.1 and
  0.3 ms, at most tripling.
  The rise follows \(z\) (2 to 1118 matches) and a larger automaton that no
  longer fits as well in cache.
- **The crossover is near \(k=10\).** For one or two patterns, building a
  trie with ordered maps costs more than KMP's array.
- **Steps per character stay low.** Transitions climb from 0.01 to 0.69
  per character, well under the bound of 2. The bound is loose here: most
  characters start no surname, so the scan stays at the root after a
  single failed lookup, which counts as neither kind of step. Failure steps
  level off at about 0.16 per character.
- **Shared prefixes save states.** The automaton has 3094 states for 4580
  pattern bytes, below the \(1+m\) bound because surnames share prefixes,
  such as "Mc" and "And".

> Summary

- The automaton is the trie of the patterns, plus failure links (the longest proper suffix that is also a state) and dictionary links. A lemma about the suffixes of \(uc\) computes the failure links breadth-first in \(O(m)\).
- Taking the depth of the current state as a potential shows that a scan makes at most \(n\) goto steps and \(n\) failure steps, for \(O(n+z)\) in total, independent of the number of patterns.
- On the Titanic names, one pass with 17 honorifics extracts every title. Survival by title separates sharply: about 16% for Mr against 70% and more for Mrs and Miss, and 57% for Master, the boys.
- With a dictionary of up to 660 surnames, the automaton makes at most 0.7 transitions per character and its time at most triples. Per-pattern KMP's time grows about 500-fold, in proportion to \(k\), and loses from about 10 patterns up.

> References

- A. V. Aho, M. J. Corasick (1975). Efficient string matching: an aid to bibliographic search. *Communications of the ACM* 18(6), 333\-\-340.
- D. E. Knuth, J. H. Morris, V. R. Pratt (1977). Fast pattern matching in strings. *SIAM Journal on Computing* 6(2), 323\-\-350.
- D. Gusfield (1997). *Algorithms on Strings, Trees, and Sequences*, section 3.4. Cambridge University Press.
